# a declared glyph operator is a function: its typed definitions are overloads a call picks by its operands' types (card operator-overloads-by-type)
'infix operator ±(v, r) := v * 10 + r; 6 ± 3'	63
'infix operator ×(v, r) := v * 10 + r; 6 × 3'	63
'class Q { a: int }; infix operator ⊕(q: Q, r: int) := q.a + r; infix operator ⊕(q: int, r: int) := q * r; [Q(1) ⊕ 2, 3 ⊕ 4]'	[3, 12]
'class Q { a: int }; infix operator ⊕(q: Q, r: int) := q.a + r; infix operator ⊕(q: int, r: int) := q * r; f(x) := x ⊕ 2; [f(Q(1)), f(3)]'	[3, 6]
# a standard module's glyph operator brings its module as its words do: ± is uncertain's (notes/plus_minus.md)
'str(6.0 ± 0.3)'	'6.00 ± 0.30'
# on a quantity ± is units': a plain spread counts in the value's unit, a quantity's converts (P220)
'str(6.0m ± 0.3)'	'6.00 ± 0.30m'
'str(5m ± 1cm)'	'5.000 ± 0.010m'
'str((5m ± 1cm) + 20cm)'	'5.200 ± 0.010m'
'str((6m ± 0.3).low)'	'5.7m'
# a glyph definition on built-in types overloads the operator for them: `[1 2 3 4] > 2` asks whether all are greater (card greater-list-all)
'>(a: list, b: int) := all a > b; [[1, 2, 3, 4] > 2, [3, 4] > 2, 5 > 2]'	[no, yes, yes]
# so does its English word (decision operator-words-top-level); a definition of numbers stays a plain function
'greater(a: list, b: int) := all a > b; [[1, 2, 3, 4] > 2, [3, 4] > 2, 5 > 2]'	[no, yes, yes]
'plus(a: list, b: int) := a.map(x => x + b); [1, 2] + 1'	[2, 3]
'greater(a: int, b: int) := 7; [greater(1, 2), 3 > 2]'	[7, yes]
